  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 259 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe dissolution of the upper level ridge previously centered over \nthe high plains brought west to northwesterly winds aloft this \nmorning and interrupted the flow of monsoonal moisture until further \nnotice. However, precipitable water (PW) values remain around 1.0\" \neast of the Colorado mountains, which is still around the 70th-85th \npercentile of climatology. At the same time, surface winds have \nremained more northwesterly than expected, in part due to slight \ndownsloping, which has caused dewpoints to remain in the low to \nmid 50s across the Denver metro and east Colorado plains. Any \nstorms that develop over the foothills and Palmer Divide will \nlikely be high-based, with the primary hazard being gusty outflows\nup to 45 mph. An isolated strong thunderstorm cannot be ruled out\nover the eastern Colorado plains, particularly in the late \nafternoon, where slightly better moisture (1.2\"-1.4\" PWs) and \ninstability (MLCAPE 700-1000 J/kg) will reside. Unlike yesterday \nafternoon, we expect storm motion to be much faster due to cloud \nlayer mean winds around 30KT.\n\nFor tomorrow, there is quite a bit of uncertainty regarding the \nshower and thunderstorm potential due to the expected shortwave and \nfrontal passage in the early morning hours. On one hand, the \nshortwave will bring additional lift. However, some high-res model \nguidance is suggesting the development of low stratus, which would \ninhibit convective initiation until the late afternoon or early \nevening. If low clouds are able to clear out early enough and \nsurface winds become south or southeasterly, there will be a decent \nchance (20%-30%) for a few isolated strong to severe thunderstorms \nto develop. The primary hazard with any storms that develop will be \ngusty outflows up to 40-50 mph. While locally heavy rainfall may \noccur with the strongest storms, we are not overly concerned \nabout flash flooding due to the fast storm motions in place, and \ngiven PW values down to about average for the time of year\n(~0.75\").\n\nThe upper level ridge will redevelop over the Four Corners this \nSaturday and Sunday and give way to another weekend of scorching hot \ntemperatures, likely reaching 100 over eastern plains and parts of \nthe Denver metro for Sunday and Monday. Heat advisory criteria will \nlikely be met on either or both days.\n\nGlobal models suggest an amplified upper level trough tracking \nthrough northern Montana late Monday night and with it a moderately \nstrong cold front for the time of year. It is difficult to tell at \nthis point whether enough moisture for significant rainfall to occur \nwill accompany this front. At the very least, this front should \nbring a day of heat relief on Tuesday with afternoon temperatures \nlikely only reaching the mid to upper 80s over the low elevations. Daily\nchances for afternoon showers and storms return on Wednesday, \nwith long-range ensembles hinting at another round of monsoonal \nmoisture on the way for late next week and into the weekend.",
